        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">For most homes, buy the Teeter FreeStep LT3</strong>, or the LT1 if
            the seat upgrades do not matter to you and the LT7 if you want a 350 lb rating and a lock that
            keeps the machine still while you get in. If getting in and out is the hard part, a swivel seat
            is worth paying for: the NuStep T5 is the clinic standard, and the HCI PhysioStep LXT offers the
            same idea for less, with less certainty about some figures. The Sunny is a fair budget choice if
            a bike-style motion with moving arms is enough.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever you choose, check the motion rather than the name, plan for the larger footprint, and
            start gently. If the machine is part of rehabilitation, let your physiotherapist set the plan. For
            an older user weighing seated against standing machines, our guide to the
            <a href="/best-ellipticals-for-seniors/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best ellipticals for seniors</a>
            compares both.
          </p>
